## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to quantify cross-cutting costs for a telemetry pipeline under real-world constraints (mobile energy, network variability, privacy).

## Key Concepts
- Cost-benefit modeling for streaming telemetry
- Adaptive sampling and batching trade-offs
- On-device resources vs backend costs
- Privacy, regulatory, and consent considerations
- Rollout strategy and break-even analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
function computeCBA(params){
  const {events, batchSize, rate, energyPerEvent, dataCost} = params;
  const batches = Math.ceil(events / batchSize);
  const energy = batches * energyPerEvent * (rate/100);
  const data = events * dataCost * (rate/100);
  return {energy, data, batches};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would opt-in rate and drift affect the CBA over time?
- How would you validate and adjust thresholds in production?

## Why This Is Asked
This question explores a privacy-preserving on-device machine learning use case with real-world constraints including energy efficiency, latency requirements, data minimization principles, and regulatory compliance. It emphasizes the importance of measurable go/no-go criteria and a concrete break-even timeline for implementation decisions.

## Key Concepts
- On-device machine learning models (autoencoder/isolation forest)
- Energy and performance budgeting for background tasks
- Data minimization and privacy impact assessments
- Go/No-Go criteria and break-even horizon analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
function detectAnomaly(readings, threshold){
  const mean = readings.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)/readings.length;
  const variance = readings.reduce((a,b)=>a+Math.pow(b-mean,2),0)/readings.length;
  return Math.abs(readings[readings.length-1] - mean) > threshold * Math.sqrt(variance);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to translate connectivity challenges into a minimal, privacy-conscious on-device flow with cost trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Edge batching, offline queues, deduping
- Energy/memory budgeting, latency impact
- Privacy, consent, rollout strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
function batchRequests(requests, maxBatch) {
  const batches = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < requests.length; i += maxBatch) {
    batches.push(requests.slice(i, i + maxBatch));
  }
  return batches;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle failed batches in poor connectivity differently for iOS vs Android?
- What telemetry would you collect to validate break-even without compromising privacy?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about nuanced trade-offs in privacy-first on-device inference with mixed offload, model update cadence, and rollout under regulatory constraints across devices and regions.

## Key Concepts

- On-device causal inference DAG
- Edge vs cloud offload costs
- Privacy/compliance budgeting
- Model update strategy and drift
- Rollout planning and break-even

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-cost estimator
function estimateCBA(params){
  const {ramPerNode, latency, inferencesPerDay, offloadKbPerDay, cloudCostPerKb, energyPerInference} = params;
  const deviceCost = ramPerNode * 0.1 + energyPerInference * inferencesPerDay;
  const offloadCost = offloadKbPerDay * cloudCostPerKb;
  const privacyOverhead = 0.08;
  const total = deviceCost + offloadCost * (1 + privacyOverhead);
  return {total, privacyOverhead};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you model drift costs and retraining triggers?
- What monitoring would you use to validate break-even?
